NAME
       resize2fs - grow or shrink an ext2, ext3 or ext4 file system

SYNOPSIS
       resize2fs [-f] [-F] [-M] [-P] [-p] DEVICE [SIZE]

DESCRIPTION
       resize2fs changes the size of an ext file system. Without SIZE it
       grows the file system to fill its device, which is what you want
       after lvextend. It can grow a mounted file system (online). Shrinking
       works only when the file system is unmounted, and resize2fs wants a
       fresh e2fsck -f first.

       SIZE is a number of blocks, or a number with a unit: K, M, G.

       For XFS, use xfs_growfs instead. lvextend -r and lvresize -r run the
       right tool for you.

OPTIONS
       -f     Force: skip some safety checks.

       -F     Flush the device's buffers first.

       -M     Shrink the file system to the smallest size it can have.

       -P     Print the smallest size the file system could shrink to.

       -p     Show progress.

EXAMPLES
       Grow an ext4 LV and its file system:

           lvextend -L +500M /dev/vgdata/archive
           resize2fs /dev/vgdata/archive

       Shrink an unmounted ext4 file system to 600 MiB:

           umount /archive
           e2fsck -f /dev/vgdata/archive
           resize2fs /dev/vgdata/archive 600M

SEE ALSO
       lvextend(8), lvresize(8), e2fsck(8), mke2fs(8), xfs_growfs(8)
